# Testing & Quality Overview

Every production change needs meaningful tests and current evidence. This section explains how to write them, measure the required scope, and run the appropriate suites on Enterprise-approved infrastructure.

**Enterprise requirement:** account for every production file and enforce **100% coverage within the approved production scope and supported metrics**. A percentage does not replace assertions, review, security assessment, or physical-device testing. Missing reports and unexecuted required suites block completion.

> **Confidential — internal use only.** Use approved systems and test data. Do not commit or share test passwords, access keys, private recordings, account information, or private artifact URLs. Organization names in reusable examples are **Enterprise**.

## Start with the right layer

| Layer | Question it answers | Android | iOS | React Native |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Unit | Does one rule behave correctly for success, failure, and boundaries? | JUnit; controlled coroutine dependencies where needed. | XCTest or the project's approved Swift Testing setup. | Jest for pure functions and reducers. |
| Integration | Do collaborating components preserve the contract? | Repository/data adapters and state owners with controlled dependencies. | Repository/state/persistence boundaries with controlled dependencies. | Real Redux Toolkit store plus controlled API/storage adapters. |
| UI/component | Can the user interact and see the expected state? | Compose UI tests or Espresso for View UI. | XCUITest for application UI. | React Native Testing Library for components; native interaction also needs device tests. |
| Device end-to-end | Does the packaged app complete a real journey on the selected device/OS? | Instrumentation through the approved Espresso execution path. | Device app and XCUITest runner. | Separate Android/iOS Appium sessions using WebdriverIO. |
| Static/security | Does the candidate meet separate code and security policy? | Lint/static checks and applicable assessment. | Compiler/static checks and applicable assessment. | Type/lint checks and native assessment. |

The table describes testing responsibilities, not a newly mandated dependency version. Preserve the approved project toolchain. React Native remains preferred for new apps, with **Redux Toolkit** for Redux state.

## Follow this sequence for each feature

1. Write acceptance criteria before writing or generating tests.
2. Identify state, I/O, authentication, cancellation, and platform boundaries.
3. Add fast tests with controlled clocks, dispatchers, repositories, and fixtures.
4. Test the actual state/UI integration, including loading, failure, and stale-result behavior where applicable.
5. Add the important packaged-app journey using stable selectors and bounded waits.
6. Run local checks and inspect both assertions and coverage gaps.
7. Submit reviewed source/tests through the established application PR process.
8. Let Bitrise execute the committed tests and dispatch approved cloud suites.
9. Inspect completed device jobs and reports; distinguish submission, skip, infrastructure failure, and assertion failure.

