            counter = "LINE"
            value = "COVEREDRATIO"
            minimum = "1.0".toBigDecimal()
        }
        limit {
            counter = "BRANCH"
            value = "COVEREDRATIO"
            minimum = "1.0".toBigDecimal()
        }
    }
}
```

Wire the configured verification task into the required check path explicitly. Verify that all intended classes are included and missing execution data cannot produce success. This fragment alone does not establish either condition. See [Gradle JaCoCo](https://docs.gradle.org/current/userguide/jacoco_plugin.html).

The [JaCoCo counter definitions](https://www.jacoco.org/jacoco/trunk/doc/counters.html) describe what is measured; exception handling is not treated as branch coverage. Test error paths even if the branch counter is already complete.

The actual Android module/plugin versions and existing coverage task definitions were not supplied. Bind the report/verification tasks in the approved template rather than copying an incompatible universal AGP configuration.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="iOS">

In the test scheme/test plan, enable coverage for every production target in scope. Confirm that extensions/frameworks are included rather than just the app target. [Apple's coverage instructions](https://help.apple.com/xcode/mac/current/en.lproj/dev9e0e09978.html) explain collection and inspection.

On an approved Mac with a shared test-enabled scheme, use a **new** result-bundle path for this run:

```bash
set -euo pipefail
: "${MOBILE_IOS_WORKSPACE:?Actual workspace path required}"
: "${MOBILE_IOS_SCHEME:?Shared scheme required}"
: "${MOBILE_SIMULATOR_UDID:?Approved simulator required}"
: "${MOBILE_RESULT_BUNDLE:?New result bundle path required}"
mkdir -p .tmp
xcodebuild \
  -workspace "$MOBILE_IOS_WORKSPACE" \
  -scheme "$MOBILE_IOS_SCHEME" \
  -destination "platform=iOS Simulator,id=$MOBILE_SIMULATOR_UDID" \
  -enableCodeCoverage YES \
  -resultBundlePath "$MOBILE_RESULT_BUNDLE" \
  test
xcrun xccov view --report --json "$MOBILE_RESULT_BUNDLE" > .tmp/ios-coverage.json
```

The command creates project-local `.tmp/` for the JSON output. Keep it ignored by Git. Use `-project` where appropriate. `MOBILE_...` values are example-owned inputs, not vendor defaults. Check the pinned Xcode's `xcrun xccov help` for its accepted invocation. Apple describes programmatic xccov access in [Testing in Xcode](https://developer.apple.com/videos/play/wwdc2019/413/).

The command produces line coverage; it does not fail merely because coverage is below 100%. A required verifier must compare every inventoried production file with report counters. Missing targets/files or zero usable evidence must block. The local helper below supports xccov's file line counters.

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

## Step 3 — Verify file completeness as well as percentages

A report that omits an untested file can look green. The helper below derives expected files from **tracked source under reviewed production roots**, then compares every expected file with report counters. New tracked files inside those roots automatically enter the gate.

The scope file is a **documentation-owned configuration**, not an Enterprise API contract. It must cover every shipped module/target, entry point, and source language. The script cannot discover that a new top-level directory is production; changes to build targets and source roots require review. Run it from the repository root on the identified source commit.

### Configure the actual source scope

Use a separate scope file for each platform. These layouts are examples; replace paths/targets with your repository's actual ones. Include only roots that exist and contain tracked files. Add handwritten native modules and extensions instead of assuming React Native means JavaScript only.

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="React Native scope">

**File: `testing/coverage-scope.rn.json`.** This example assumes both listed entry files exist. Edit the entry list to match the real app.

```json
{
  "source_roots": ["src"],
  "suffixes": [".js", ".jsx", ".ts", ".tsx"],
  "entry_files": ["App.tsx", "index.js"],
  "classifications": {}
}
```

Tests colocated under `src/`, `.d.ts` files, and tracked generated/vendor files must be listed by exact path with a reviewed classification reason. For example, classify an actual `src/features/balance/signInAndLoadBalance.test.ts` as test code. Do not classify the production `signInAndLoadBalance.ts` out of coverage.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="Android scope">

**File: `testing/coverage-scope.android.json`.** Example for a Kotlin source root; add existing Java, variant, and other module roots as needed.

```json
{
  "source_roots": ["app/src/main/kotlin"],
  "suffixes": [".kt", ".java"],
  "entry_files": [],
  "classifications": {}
}
```

The helper maps JaCoCo package/source filenames into these source roots. If two modules have the same package/source path, it fails on ambiguity: run each module's report against its own reviewed scope or supply an approved unambiguous aggregation. Kotlin package paths must match this mapping; alternate layouts need a reviewed path adapter.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="iOS scope">

**File: `testing/coverage-scope.ios.json`.** Replace the source root and xccov report target name. Include all production extensions/frameworks as applicable.

```json
{
  "source_roots": ["Mobile"],
  "suffixes": [".swift", ".m", ".mm", ".c", ".cpp"],
  "entry_files": [],
  "classifications": {},
  "xccov_targets": ["Mobile.app"]
}
```

Target names must exactly match the report from your pinned Xcode. Test targets do not substitute for missing production targets. Headers, resources, target membership, and build settings still need their separate inventory/classification; this example checks executable source, not every file format.

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

`classifications` is an exact-path-to-reason object for files assessed separately, such as tests, generated/vendor code, or verified non-executable declarations. Reasons need the team's approval/reference. A classification removes a file from executable checking, so review changes like gate-policy changes. It must never become a bypass for handwritten executable production code. Stale/out-of-scope classifications fail.

### Add the verifier

**File: `scripts/testing/check-coverage.py`.** Python 3 standard library only; requires Git and the current tracked source. The approved CI runtime supplies both.

```python
import argparse
import json
import subprocess
from pathlib import Path
import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET

parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
parser.add_argument('--format', choices=['jest', 'jacoco', 'xccov'], required=True)
parser.add_argument('--report', required=True)
parser.add_argument('--scope', required=True)
args = parser.parse_args()
root = Path.cwd().resolve()
git_root = subprocess.run(['git', 'rev-parse', '--show-toplevel'],
                          check=True, capture_output=True, text=True).stdout.strip()
if Path(git_root).resolve() != root:
    raise SystemExit('Run from the repository root')

def stop(message):
    raise SystemExit(message)

def canonical(value):
    path = Path(value)
    absolute = path.resolve() if path.is_absolute() else (root / path).resolve()
    return absolute.relative_to(root).as_posix()

scope = json.loads(Path(args.scope).read_text())
roots = scope['source_roots']
suffixes = scope['suffixes']
entries = scope.get('entry_files', [])
exclusions = scope.get('classifications', {})
if not roots or not suffixes:
    stop('Declare all production roots and source suffixes')
for value in [*roots, *entries, *exclusions]:
    if canonical(value) != value:
        stop('Scope paths must be canonical and repository-relative')
if not all(isinstance(reason, str) and reason.strip() for reason in exclusions.values()):
    stop('Every classification needs a reviewed reason')
tracked = subprocess.run(['git', 'ls-files', '-z'], check=True, capture_output=True).stdout
tracked = set(tracked.decode('utf-8').split('\0')) - {''}
if not set(entries) <= tracked:
    stop('Declared entry file missing from tracked source')
for folder in roots:
    if not any(name.startswith(folder + '/') for name in tracked):
        stop('Production root has no tracked files: ' + folder)
candidates = {name for name in tracked if name.endswith(tuple(suffixes)) and (
    name in entries or any(name.startswith(folder + '/') for folder in roots))}
if not set(exclusions) <= candidates:
    stop('Classification is stale or outside declared source scope')
expected = candidates - set(exclusions)
if not expected or any(canonical(name) != name or not (root / name).is_file() for name in expected):
    stop('No usable production inventory or tracked file missing on disk')

files = {}
def add(name, counters):
    try:
        key = canonical(name)
    except ValueError:
        return  # A required file will still fail as missing below.
    if key not in expected:
        return
    if key in files:
        stop('Duplicate report file; use a reviewed merge/target map: ' + key)
    files[key] = counters

if args.format == 'jacoco':
    document = ET.parse(args.report).getroot()
    for package in document.findall('.//package'):
        for source in package.findall('sourcefile'):
            relative = '/'.join(filter(None, [package.attrib['name'], source.attrib['name']]))
            matches = [folder + '/' + relative for folder in roots
                       if folder + '/' + relative in expected]
            if len(matches) > 1:
                stop('Ambiguous Android source mapping; split module reports')
            if matches:
                # JaCoCo omits counters whose total is zero.
                counters = {name: {'total': 0, 'covered': 0}
                            for name in ['LINE', 'BRANCH', 'METHOD', 'INSTRUCTION']}
                seen = set()
                for counter in source.findall('counter'):
                    metric = counter.attrib['type']
                    if metric in seen:
                        stop('Duplicate JaCoCo metric')
                    seen.add(metric)
                    covered, missed = int(counter.attrib['covered']), int(counter.attrib['missed'])
                    if covered < 0 or missed < 0:
                        stop('Invalid JaCoCo counters')
                    counters[metric] = {'total': covered + missed, 'covered': covered}
                add(matches[0], counters)
    metrics = ['LINE', 'BRANCH', 'METHOD', 'INSTRUCTION']
else:
    document = json.loads(Path(args.report).read_text())
    if args.format == 'jest':
        for name, counters in document.items():
            if name != 'total':
                add(name, counters)
        metrics = ['statements', 'branches', 'functions', 'lines']
    else:
        targets = scope['xccov_targets']
        if not targets or len(targets) != len(set(targets)):
            stop('Declare unique production xccov target names')
        selected = [target for target in document['targets'] if target['name'] in targets]
        if sorted(target['name'] for target in selected) != sorted(targets):
            stop('Missing or duplicate required xccov target')
        for target in selected:
            for file in target['files']:
                add(file['path'], {'lines': {
                    'total': file['executableLines'], 'covered': file['coveredLines']}})
        metrics = ['lines']

failures = []
for name in sorted(expected):
    counters = files.get(name)
    if counters is None:
        failures.append(name + ': missing from report')
        continue
    usable = False
    for metric in metrics:
        values = counters.get(metric, {})
        total, covered = values.get('total'), values.get('covered')
        if (type(total) is not int or type(covered) is not int or
                total < 0 or covered < 0 or covered > total):
            failures.append(name + ': invalid ' + metric + ' counters')
            continue
        usable = usable or total > 0
        if covered != total:
            failures.append(name + ': uncovered ' + metric)
    primary = 'LINE' if args.format == 'jacoco' else 'lines'
    if not usable or counters.get(primary, {}).get('total', 0) == 0:
        failures.append(name + ': no executable evidence; classify explicitly')
if failures:
    stop('Coverage gate failed:\n' + '\n'.join(failures))
print(f'Coverage gate passed: {len(expected)} files; {len(exclusions)} classified separately')
```

The helper supports Jest JSON summary, **JaCoCo XML**, and selected-target **xccov JSON**. It does not claim compatibility with arbitrary Kover/export schemas. Pin and verify the report producer. Missing/corrupt report structures exit unsuccessfully rather than become success.

For a verified JaCoCo report, omitted counters represent zero totals under the [JaCoCo XML writer contract](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jacoco/jacoco/master/org.jacoco.report/src/org/jacoco/report/internal/xml/XMLCoverageWriter.java). A file still needs executable line evidence. Zero branches means no measured branch opportunities; it is not a reason to manufacture a 100% branch count. Exception-handler coverage also needs meaningful tests beyond the counter definition.

### Execute the gate for each platform

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="React Native gate">

After the configured Jest coverage run:

```bash
python3 scripts/testing/check-coverage.py \
  --format jest \
  --report coverage/coverage-summary.json \
  --scope testing/coverage-scope.rn.json
```

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="Android gate">

After the configured Android tests and XML report generation:

```bash
set -euo pipefail
: "${MOBILE_ANDROID_COVERAGE_XML:?Actual JaCoCo XML path required}"
python3 scripts/testing/check-coverage.py \
  --format jacoco \
  --report "$MOBILE_ANDROID_COVERAGE_XML" \
  --scope testing/coverage-scope.android.json
```

The report path is an example-owned input. Generate it through the actual approved Gradle task; assigning a path does not create coverage. The helper checks line, branch, method, and instruction counters for all mapped in-scope source files. Inspect tool-generated/filtered code and scope treatment before interpreting the metric as handwritten source coverage.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="iOS gate">

After the earlier Xcode collection/export:

```bash
python3 scripts/testing/check-coverage.py \
  --format xccov \
  --report .tmp/ios-coverage.json \
  --scope testing/coverage-scope.ios.json
```

The helper checks line counters, including required production targets. Reports must use this checkout's source paths; a different path prefix requires a reviewed normalizer. Duplicate mappings fail rather than guessing how to merge different targets/runs.

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

The helper verifies **file presence and counters**, not report authenticity or candidate identity. The protected workflow must generate fresh reports after actual tests, associate them with the exact commit/run/target, and reject stale or substituted evidence. Build configuration and scope are both reviewed inputs.

## Step 4 — Make the result a required gate

The template must execute real tests, collect fresh coverage, run the verifier, and preserve its exit status. Required status checks must point to the current candidate revision. A successful artifact upload does not turn a failed test into success.

```yaml
# Bitrise excerpt: npm project with approved tooling already prepared.
- script@1:
    title: Execute tests and enforce production coverage
    inputs:
      - content: |-
          #!/usr/bin/env bash
          set -euo pipefail
          npm run test:coverage
          python3 scripts/testing/check-coverage.py             --format jest             --report coverage/coverage-summary.json             --scope testing/coverage-scope.rn.json
```

A required gate must preserve these distinctions:

| Evidence | Required decision |
| :--- | :--- |
| Tests fail, even with 100% coverage | Block. Assertions still failed. |
| Coverage fails, even with passing tests | Block. The production coverage requirement is unmet. |
| Coverage passes but required device suite is incomplete | Device gate remains blocked/incomplete. |
| Coverage and device checks pass but security/approval is missing | Remaining release gate stays blocked. |

Only use the Python binding provisioned by the approved runner. Native workflows invoke their actual configured test/report/verifier tasks, with the same fail-closed requirement. Configure failure-path evidence collection separately without swallowing the original failure.

## Step 5 — Prove the gate fails correctly

| Deliberate fixture/change | Expected behavior |
| :--- | :--- |
| One production file is not imported by any test | Included with uncovered code; gate fails. |
| Required file missing from report | Gate fails. |
| Missing/corrupt report or empty inventory | Gate fails. |
| Covered count below total | Gate fails. |
| Metric is unsupported | Do not fabricate it; use approved metric mapping and record the limitation. |
| Required suite is skipped or discovers no tests | Required test evidence is incomplete, regardless of percentage. |
| New tracked file under a production root has no report entry | The derived inventory includes it; the gate fails. |
| New production root/target was added | Review and extend scope, report targets, and collection; the helper cannot infer build ownership. |
| Old report copied into this build | Reject through revision/run provenance checks. |

Store source commit, report tool/configuration, production inventory/classification, suite results, metrics, and verifier result with the build evidence. Do not claim a synthetic helper check proves complete native application coverage.

**Next: [Sauce Labs Real-Device Testing](../sauce-labs-real-device-testing/)** — device packages, Enterprise request YAML, runner integration, and evidence.


## Inspect and record the result

Open the report's uncovered files/lines, then identify whether the cause is a missing behavior test, incorrect source collection, a missing target, or an ambiguous mapping. Fix the real cause; do not remove the file, weaken thresholds, or fabricate counters.

Record the source commit, build/run reference, tool versions, test counts, scope file revision, required target set, report references, and verifier result. For split native reports, retain every required module's independent outcome. An overall average must not hide a failing module.

**Attach screenshot — platform coverage report showing the intended targets/files and supported metrics, with sensitive paths/source details redacted.**

**Attach screenshot — required CI status and verifier result for the same candidate revision.**

### Reference verification

The MDX page compiled, all six standalone Bash snippets passed syntax checking, and JSON examples parsed. The inline helper passed 20 synthetic Git/report cases across Jest, JaCoCo XML, and xccov JSON, including missing files/targets, uncovered counters, new tracked files, classifications, duplicate paths, and Android mapping ambiguity.

These checks validate the helper's tested decisions, not actual app coverage. Jest, Gradle/AGP coverage generation, Xcode collection, Bitrise execution, and site diagram rendering were not exercised. Validate the actual pinned report formats, source scope, and target/task integration in your project.

## Completion checklist

- [ ] Every shipped production root, entry point, source language, and target is accounted for.
- [ ] Separately assessed/generated/vendor files have exact reviewed classifications.
- [ ] Unimported production files enter coverage collection.
- [ ] Required tests execute and report meaningful assertions; skipped/empty suites do not pass.
- [ ] Correct native/JavaScript metrics are enforced at 100% without relabeling unsupported metrics.
- [ ] Missing files, ambiguous mapping, corrupt counters, and missing targets fail the gate.
- [ ] Fresh reports and scope configuration are associated with the exact candidate and run.
- [ ] Required statuses cannot be bypassed by a successful build or report upload.
- [ ] Failure evidence is retained without exposing secrets or source to unauthorized audiences.




