# Real-Device Cloud Testing with Sauce Labs

Use Sauce Labs to execute reviewed mobile tests against the **identified application build on an approved physical-device matrix**. A successful upload is preparation; a passing device gate requires completed suites, meaningful assertions, and matching result evidence.

This page explains the Enterprise two-step Bitrise integration from the supplied reference. [Writing Tests](../writing-tests/) contains the Android, iOS, and React Native login-to-balance examples. [Coverage & Quality Gates](../coverage-and-quality-gates/) separately enforces production coverage.

> **Confidential — internal use only.** Use synthetic test data and approved accounts. Keep Sauce access keys, application credentials, private binaries, account data, recordings, and private artifact URLs within approved systems. Redact diagnostics before sharing. Organization names in examples are **Enterprise**.

## 1. Understand what runs where

| Location | Responsibility |
| :--- | :--- |
| Developer IDE/local test target | Author and review tests, verify selectors, and run the permitted local emulator/Simulator smoke test. |
| Bitrise | Build the selected artifacts, bind protected credentials, execute the approved runner, and collect gate evidence. |
| Sauce Labs | Allocate supported devices and execute the selected native instrumentation or Appium session. |
| Enterprise platform adapter | Interpret `saucelabs.request.yml`, maintain the supplied step contract, associate uploads/jobs, and reconcile uncertain writes. |

Under the supplied policy, **cloud execution starts through Bitrise**, not ad hoc Sauce portal sessions or developer-triggered local cloud runs. Portal access can be used for authorized inspection of results. Local emulator/Simulator authoring checks remain distinct from cloud execution.

Real-device tests reveal behavior that virtual targets can miss. They do not eliminate OEM crashes or guarantee compatibility with every device. Confirm device availability and support for the required sensors, biometrics, peripherals, signing, and app capabilities before promising coverage of that feature.

