## 9. Read CI and cloud results accurately

After the approved source event starts the configured checks, Bitrise executes committed code. It does not regenerate the test to fit a failure.

| Boundary | Evidence to inspect |
| :--- | :--- |
| Checkout | Exact commit being evaluated; it may be a configured PR merge candidate rather than the local branch head. |
| Tests/coverage | Executed suite/test counts, real assertion results, source inventory, supported metrics, and gate result. |
| Packaging | Native app and required test-runner packages, source/build identity, and hashes. |
| Device dispatch | Verified storage/job associations, framework, approved matrix, and region. |
| Device completion | Actual device/OS and completed required suite results. |
| Reporting | Sanitized diagnostics and current required statuses associated with that candidate. |

Android instrumentation uses the matched application/test APKs; XCUITest needs supported device app/runner packaging; React Native Appium uses separate Android/iOS app candidates and a WebdriverIO suite. Reuse the actual adapter contracts from the Sauce Labs page rather than assuming one artifact or ID works for both platforms.

No-device availability, no tests discovered, missing reports, incomplete jobs, or skipped required suites remain incomplete/failing evidence. Follow [Coverage & Quality Gates](../coverage-and-quality-gates/) and [Sauce Labs](../sauce-labs-real-device-testing/) for enforcement. Keep security assessment, signing, release authorization, and user-visible distribution separate from test success.

If registration, upload, or job creation has an uncertain outcome, preserve the identities and use read-only reconciliation. Do not ask the assistant to repeat an external mutation until it succeeds. The Enterprise one-write rule still applies.

